# Expectation record for 14_abi_calling_convention/002_many_float_parameters.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_many_float_parameters
area               = 14_abi_calling_convention
description        = Floating parameter lists long enough to exhaust every target's floating argument registers and force stack spill: fifteen double parameters and fifteen float parameters -- fourteen observed floating values plus a trailing int variant tag in each list -- each list called in a folded and a volatile-runtime variant, with every one of the fourteen floating parameters printed individually
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction:
- Fourteen floating parameters plus a trailing integer tag are passed to each callee, which
  exhausts the widest floating argument register file of the four supported ABIs - eight on
  System V AMD64 (xmm0-xmm7), AAPCS64 (v0-v7) and LP64D (fa0-fa7) - and continues past that
  threshold, so every target marshals part of the list beyond its floating registers. On the
  three 64-bit targets the trailing integer also passes through the integer allocator while
  the floating allocator is already full. Where the six excess floating values go differs
  between the four ABIs: three spill them to the stack, while RISC-V LP64D passes them in the
  INTEGER argument registers, which is a distinct and more error-prone path - and a reason
  every one of the fourteen FLOATING parameters is printed back individually and in order
  rather than summarised. The fifteenth parameter is the int tag; it appears in the output as
  the variant name every line of the group carries rather than as a value of its own, so a
  tag delivered to the wrong slot renames every line of the group and is equally visible.
- Every literal is a dyadic rational with a denominator of at most 16, so it is exactly
  representable in both floating types and prints exactly at four decimal places with no
  rounding tie and no rounding decision anywhere.

- No signed integer overflow: every integer value passed or printed is a small constant or a
  value read from initialised storage, far from INT_MIN and INT_MAX; the long long values are
  likewise far inside their range.
- No shift operator appears, so no shift count can be out of range.
- No floating operation can overflow, underflow, divide by zero or produce a not-a-number or
  an infinity: every floating value is a small dyadic rational and the only operations
  performed on them are copying and printing.
- No read of uninitialised storage: every object is initialised at its declaration or by an
  assignment that precedes every read of it, and every volatile object has a static
  initialiser.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points.
- Nothing depends on padding bytes: sizeof is never printed, no aggregate is compared
  byte-wise, and only named members are ever read.
- Nothing depends on the relative addresses of unrelated objects, and no address or pointer
  value is printed.
- Every float value reaching printf is cast to double explicitly, so the default argument
  promotion is visible rather than implicit, and every conversion specification matches its
  argument's type after promotion.
- The two-variant rule is applied throughout: a folded variant whose arguments are
  compile-time constants, and a runtime variant whose arguments are read from volatile
  storage so the values are genuinely materialised and marshalled rather than folded into the
  callee. Without the second variant, optimisation would substitute the folder's answer for
  the backend's at -O1 and -O2 and an argument-marshalling defect would escape entirely.
- Nothing is allocated, nothing outside the translation unit is read, and main returns 0.

- NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION ORDER, and it is enforced structurally rather
  than argued. An access to a volatile object is an observable side effect, and C11
  6.5.2.2p10 leaves the order of evaluation of a call's function designator, of its
  arguments, and of subexpressions within those arguments unspecified. So the runtime
  variants STAGE every volatile datum before any call: the tag is copied into a plain int in
  a statement of its own, and each element of vdbl and vflt is copied into plain array
  storage in a bounded loop, one volatile read per iteration and per full expression. The
  calls then pass only those plain objects. The one remaining volatile access in a call
  expression is the call-boundary function pointer itself, which is a single access and
  therefore has no order to be unspecified. Staging costs no discriminating power: a value
  that arrived through a volatile load stays opaque to the optimizer, so every argument is
  still materialised at run time and marshalled through the ABI.

Verified empirically on this branch with gcc 13.4.0 and qemu-user 10.1.0: the default
strict warning gate (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
-Werror) produces zero diagnostics at -O0, -O1 and -O2; the sanitizer gate
(-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all) runs to completion with no
output and exit status 0; and all twelve cells of the declared matrix produce
byte-identical stdout with exit status 0 and an empty standard error.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This area exists because the four backends implement four different application binary
interfaces, and it is the densest concentration of cross-backend divergence surface in the
corpus. The parameter and aggregate counts are sized against the WIDEST register file rather
than the narrowest - eight integer registers on AAPCS64 (x0-x7) and LP64D (a0-a7), six on
System V AMD64 (rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9), and none on System V i386 cdecl, which marshals
everything on the stack - so every target genuinely spills and no target is left exercising
only its register path.

The measured target differences are handled by construction, so no target restriction and no
marker is needed:

1. sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64
   (docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457-462). Only int, long long and double are
   passed or printed as values; no long, no size_t and no pointer value appears in the
   output.
2. Plain char signedness is implementation-defined and was measured signed on x86_64 and
   i686 and unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64. No plain char is passed or printed.
3. long double has three representations across the four targets (measured 16, 12, 16 and 16
   bytes). None is used here; that type belongs to
   13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.
4. Aggregate SIZE and LAYOUT are never printed and no aggregate is ever compared byte-wise:
   sizeof is never printed and no padding byte is read, so the fact that a mixed int-and-
   double aggregate is 12 bytes on i686, where double has four-byte alignment, and 16 bytes
   on the other three targets cannot affect the output. Only named members are read back.
5. Every floating literal is a dyadic rational with a denominator of at most 16, so it is
   exactly representable in both binary32 and binary64 and prints exactly at four decimal
   places with no rounding tie. That is what keeps the output byte-identical even on i686,
   whose reference compiler evaluates with x87 80-bit intermediates (FLT_EVAL_METHOD 2).
6. All four targets are little-endian, and no byte-order-sensitive value is printed.
END
